1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,860
If a vendor walks into your ministry today and, uh, pitches a one hundred percent unique data platform,

2
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:07,800
you should probably just show them the door.

3
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:15,680
Yeah, definitely. Because in government tech, unique usually just means you'll end up, you know, locked in to one supplier.

4
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,960
Right. And it becomes incredibly costly to change later on.

5
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:24,680
So today's deep dive is about an alternative that donors and auditors will actually trust rather than, like,

6
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:26,940
relying on some black box system.

7
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:37,160
Exactly. And the most defensible alternative you can offer your minister is a published pattern followed in the open where every architectural choice is written down.

8
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:46,060
So our mission today is exploring how taking the reference architecture from UNICEF Giga's School Master Data can actually help you build a robust learner register

9
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,080
without, you know, reinventing the wheel.

10
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,540
Right. And we hear the phrase "single source of truth" a lot,

11
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:59,240
but Giga's architecture actually stops your program's data from becoming fragmented, siloed, or duplicated.

12
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:04,860
But how exactly does it do that? I mean, how does it physically stop the duplication across different departments?

13
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:08,560
Well, it passes the data through a very specific five-tier system.

14
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:15,440
It's, uh, inspired by the medallion pattern, so you have raw, bronze, staging, silver, and gold.

15
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:21,340
Okay, so that's a lot of tiers. The raw tier is just, what, the untouched data coming in?

16
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,520
Yeah, exactly. The raw tier is all the unfiltered data.

17
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:30,000
Then the bronze tier acts as this first automated filter that catches basic formatting errors.

18
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:32,220
Oh, got it. So it's like an initial sweep.

19
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:36,900
But, I mean, you can't rely entirely on an algorithm for a national database.

20
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:38,040
You really can't.

21
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:45,340
If two separate districts upload conflicting data about the exact same student, an automated filter might just guess incorrectly.

22
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:47,240
Right, which would be disastrous.

23
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:51,220
Exactly. Which is why the data then moves to the staging tier.

24
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:57,260
Staging acts as this holding tank where a human being actually reviews the flagged information.

25
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:01,800
Oh, wow. So a person physically resolves those conflicts before it moves on.

26
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:10,120
Yep, exactly. And once that's done, it moves to the silver tier, where records are matched and consolidated, leaving us with the gold tier.

27
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:12,300
Okay, the gold tier,

28
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:15,520
the final pristine data. But wait,

29
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:21,880
I read that Giga actually splits that gold tier into two separate tables: a master table and a reference table.

30
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:22,680
They do, yeah.

31
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:26,880
Why complicate it at the very end? Like, why not just keep it in one big clean database?

32
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:31,760
Because by splitting it, you separate the core identities from the descriptive attributes.

33
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:36,920
Uh, the master table holds the unchanging identifier, like a school's permanent ID.

34
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:41,560
Oh, I see. And the reference table holds the stuff that changes, like current internet speed or whatever.

35
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:42,140
Precisely.

36
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:48,260
That structural split is what forces all departments to reference the exact same core identity,

37
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,720
which prevents those data silos we mentioned.

38
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,680
Okay, but I have to push back a little here.

39
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:58,060
A school is a static building. A learner is a human being constantly moving between districts.

40
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,820
Right. It's a very different subject.

41
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:08,720
Yeah. So if this pattern is built for static buildings, doesn't the whole architecture break if you apply it to a dynamic learner register, like in a program like Progressa?

42
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:13,060
No, it doesn't break at all, actually. You just swap out the core subject.

43
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:18,700
You keep the five tiers, the bronze checks, the human review and staging, and that gold split.

44
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:24,500
Okay, so the structure stays exactly the same, but instead of buildings, you're processing dynamic learner profiles.

45
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:30,280
Exactly. But because learners are dynamic, you do have to change the final destination.

46
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:35,000
Instead of a static gold table, the data feeds directly into a register.

47
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,080
And a register is an act of service, right?

48
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:39,600
Not just a file sitting on a server.

49
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:48,700
Right. It acts as a dynamic gatekeeper. It has very specific rules about who is allowed to read or write data about a student at any given moment.

50
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:55,620
Okay, that makes sense. But wait, if this act of service is perfectly mapped out in Giga's public repositories,

51
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:58,580
I'm a bit confused on one thing. What's that?

52
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:04,000
Why would I spend my ministry's budget to write the code from scratch if it's just sitting there publicly on GitHub?

53
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:05,140
I mean, why not just copy it?

54
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:10,960
Ah, so there is a massive catch there regarding open patterns versus open source code.

55
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:15,960
Neither of Giga's public repositories actually carries a software license.

56
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:17,399
Wait, really?

57
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:18,440
None at all?

58
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:19,279
None.

59
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,560
And in the software world, having no license means you have absolutely no legal permission to copy the code,

60
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,280
even if you can read every single line of it online.

61
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:29,620
Oh, wow.

62
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,520
So I can see the structural architecture, uh, the blueprint,

63
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:39,020
but I still have to build it myself. I can't just lift their code.

64
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:41,260
That is the perfect way to look at it, yeah.

65
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:47,000
You follow the reference architecture, and you explicitly cite the Giga pattern in your design documents.

66
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:50,420
But my internal team has to write its own code,

67
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:56,940
so when an auditor or a donor reviews the system, they see exactly where the blueprint came from?

68
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:04,660
Exactly. Which keeps you completely legally in the clear, and it helps immensely in the room where the money is decided.

69
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:12,660
Because a globally published pattern is vastly easier to defend than some mysterious architecture a vendor invented yesterday.

70
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:13,220
Right.

71
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:14,320
Absolutely.

72
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:21,420
And writing down these architectural choices ensures they survive a change of team or the end of a donor project.

73
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:25,120
Meaning your ministry isn't locked into one supplier.

74
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:30,180
The next vendor can just come in, read the documentation, and follow the exact same blueprint.

75
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:30,620
Yep.

76
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:35,560
It fundamentally changes the power dynamic, putting your ministry back in the driver's seat.

